{
  "version": 2,
  "hash": "c995b96083e4d2d2385cca7900f12a02",
  "file_hash": "c995b96083e4d2d2385cca7900f12a02",
  "translated_at": "2026-09-04T15:43:55.028898",
  "target_lang": "en",
  "chunks": [
    {
      "hash": "52c8c87372863dc5357064fe0bb4100f",
      "translation": "# Unified Control Plane (scope)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This feature requires ErisPulse **2.8.0+**.\n\nThe unified control plane answers six questions: **which modules are available, who receives events, who can execute a specific command, which text a module processes, which implementation parameters are overridden, and which outbound calls are prohibited for a module**. The control authority is entirely given to the user: at the **upper level** of module / adapter / command / processor registration (configured via `ErisPulse.scope` or at runtime via `sdk.scope`), it is declared uniformly, and the event pipeline automatically reads and executes it at each level.\n\nThe control plane consolidates the original multiple permission systems and serves as the **sole** entry point for permissions/access control in version 2.8.0:\n\n| Dimension | Controls What | Rejection Behavior | Configuration Path |\n|-----------|---------------|--------------------|--------------------|\n| **① Module** | Which modules are available (platform / Bot / session three levels) | Silent ignore (no reply, no claim) | `scope.platforms / bots / sessions` |\n| **② Identity** | Whether to receive events (adapter / Bot / session / user four levels) | Complete discard at the entry (silent) | `scope.identity.*` |\n| **③ Command** | Who can execute a specific command (command names support glob) | Reply with \"insufficient permissions\" (explicit) | `scope.commands` |\n| **④ Processor** | Event processors of a module filtered by text | No trigger (silent) | `scope.handlers` |\n| **⑤ Override** | Override implementation parameters of modules/commands (master/hidden/aliases/prefix) | —— (only changes parameters) | `scope.overrides` |\n| **⑥ Outbound Actions** | Prohibit modules from sending messages / calling standard APIs / processing requests | Failure response (`retcode=34601`) | `scope.actions` |\n\n{!--< tips >!--}\n1. Import the singleton via `from ErisPulse.Core import scope` (same object as `sdk.scope`)\n2. `scope.is_allowed(platform, bot_id, module, session_id)` checks if a module is available\n3. `scope.is_identity_allowed(platform, bot_id, session_id, user_id)` checks if an event is allowed\n4. `scope.allow_user(\"roll*\", platform, uid)` / `deny_user(...)` command ACL (supports glob)\n5. `scope.override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\", master=True)` overrides implementation parameters\n6. `scope.set_action(\"MyModule\", \"send\", False)` prohibits a module from replying/sending messages\n7. `scope.get_stats()` checks filtering statistics; `scope.get_topology()` checks topology\n{!--< /tips >!--}"
    },
    {
      "hash": "20701184cb2ef4b82c0b458cf4f3a834",
      "translation": "## Matching Entry Syntax (Unified Across the System)\n\nAll \"name lists\" in the control plane (module names, identity keys, command names) use the same matching syntax (`ErisPulse.Core.text_match`):\n\n| Syntax | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| Exact name | `\"Chat\"` | Full value comparison, **case-insensitive** |\n| Glob | `\"Tool*\"` or `\"spam_*\"` | `*` matches any string / `?` matches a single character / `[seq]` matches a character set, case-insensitive |\n| Regular expression | `\"re:^Danger.*\"` | Declared with the `re:` prefix, uses regular expression `search` matching, default case-insensitive |\n\n- Invalid regular expressions **silently degrade** to \"no match\" (no error or crash)\n- Decorator parameters (`pattern=` / `regex=`) have fixed semantics: `pattern` is a glob, `regex` is the raw regular expression source (without the `re:` prefix); regular expression entries in control plane configurations **must** include the `re:` prefix"
    },
    {
      "hash": "2e84d9f55fc7ecc46d627cd3907585fb",
      "translation": "## Global Fallback: `default_allow`\n\n`default_allow` is the **unique global fallback switch** (default `true`), which applies uniformly to all three decision dimensions:\n\n- **Module dimension**: If no binding is matched → `default_allow` determines whether to allow or deny.\n- **Identity dimension**: If no policy is matched → `default_allow` determines whether to allow or deny.\n- **Command dimension**: If no ACL is configured → `default_allow=true` delegates to the developer's default permission chain; `false` (strict mode) means unconfigured commands are denied.\n\nSetting it to `false` enables the \"implicit deny\" strict mode: whitelist-based management, where anything not explicitly allowed is denied.\n\n> **Exception**: The **outbound action dimension** is **not** affected by `default_allow` — it is an independent tightening switch, defaulting to full allow, and only explicitly set to `false` to disable (calls with an empty framework-level owner are always allowed). This ensures that a strict global mode does not accidentally cut off all module message replies."
    },
    {
      "hash": "7d1258cccf71a21461f676ac73868a51",
      "translation": "## Configuration File\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope]\ndefault_allow = true        # Global fallback (false = strict implicit deny mode)\ncache_size = 1024           # LRU cache size\n\n# ── ① Module Level (Priority: Session > Bot > Platform) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.platforms.onebot11]\nmodules = [\"Chat\", \"Tool*\"]   # Whitelist: exact name / glob / re: regex\nblocked = [\"re:^Danger\"]\n[ErisPulse.scope.bots.onebot11.\"123456\"]\nmodules = [\"Chat\"]\n[ErisPulse.scope.sessions.onebot11.\"789012345\"]\nmodules = [\"Chat\"]\n\n# ── ② Identity Level (Priority: User > Session > Bot > Adapter) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.adapters.onebot11]\ndeny = true                   # Discard all events from this adapter\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.bots.onebot11.\"123456\"]\ndeny = true\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.sessions.onebot11.\"g_blocked\"]\ndeny = true\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.users.onebot11]\nallow = [\"u_admin\"]           # User keys support glob / re: regex\ndeny = [\"u_bad\", \"spam_*\"]\n\n# ── ③ Command Level (Command names support glob) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.commands.\"roll*\"]\nallow = [\"onebot11:u_vip\"]    # User identifier \"platform:user_id\"\ndeny = [\"onebot11:u_bad\"]\n\n# ── ④ Handler/Text Level ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.handlers.MyModule]\npattern = \"签到*\"             # AND with pattern/regex conditions in code\nregex = \"re:\\\\d+\\\\s*元\"\n\n# ── ⑤ Override Implementation Parameters ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.overrides.MyModule.restart]\nmaster = true                 # Only available to framework owner\nhidden = true                 # Hide in help\naliases = [\"rs\"]              # Append alias\nprefix = \"!\"                  # Append trigger prefix\n\n# ── ⑥ Outbound Action Level (Default: all allowed, only explicit deny restricts) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.actions.MyModule]\nsend = false                  # Disable MyModule from replying/actively sending messages\napi = false                   # Disable MyModule from calling standard API (including call escape hatch)\nrequest = false               # Disable MyModule from handling request operations accept/reject\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "6e55cef536f974408c5aff7f33655a37",
      "translation": "## ① Module Level\n\nAnswer the question: \"In a given context, which modules are available?\" By default, all modules are open; filtering only begins after configuration binding.  \n**No changes are required for modules or adapters.**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Event arrives at a module's handler/command\"] --> B{\"scope.is_allowed<br/>(platform, bot, module, session)\"}\n    B --> C{\"Find active binding<br/>Session-level > Bot-level > Platform-level\"}\n    C -->|\"Matched\"| D[\"blocked matched → Reject<br/>modules non-empty → Only whitelist allowed<br/>Both empty → default_allow\"]\n    C -->|\"Not matched\"| E[\"default_allow (default true = allow)\"]\n    D -->|\"Reject\"| Z[\"Silently ignore<br/> (No reply, no claim, only TRACE log visible)\"]\n```\n\n- **Resolution Priority:** Session-level > Bot-level > Platform-level. Higher priority bindings **fully override** lower priority ones.\n- **Silent Semantics:** Commands and handlers of filtered modules are not triggered, do not reply, and are not claimed (to prevent cross-command mis-matches),  \n  and are only visible in TRACE-level logs (`core.scope.denied`).\n- **Framework-level handlers** (`scope_exempt=True` or owner is empty) are unaffected; module names that are empty (framework-level resources) are always allowed.\n- **Session-aware help and command queries:** Command query APIs (`command.help` / `get_command` / `get_commands` / `get_group_commands` / `get_visible_commands`,  \n  and `module.get_commands_overview`) all support optional `event=` or explicit  \n  `platform=` / `bot_id=` / `session_id=` keywords—commands from modules unavailable in the current session  \n  no longer appear in the results (`get_command` returns None, single command help is treated as \"unregistered\",  \n  consistent with silent semantics); if no context is provided, full behavior is maintained. The help/hidden fields returned by command queries are merged and overridden values (user priority)."
    },
    {
      "hash": "76ab0a703d7192a9f250a79750e98dfc",
      "translation": "## ② Identity Dimension (Event Admission)\n\nAnswer the question: \"Whose events are accepted or rejected?\" Events that are denied are **completely discarded at the distribution entry**—they do not enter any middleware or processor (including framework-level), and are only visible in TRACE-level logs (`core.scope.identity_denied`).\n\n- **Resolution priority: User > Session > Bot > Adapter**, taking the most specific configured policy; deny takes precedence over allow\n- Each level is bound to a binary policy: `{ allow = true }` or `{ deny = true }`\n- User keys support glob / regular expressions (e.g., `\"spam_*\"` to block a batch of spam users)\n- Typical usage—deny at the upper level and allow for specific users to create \"exceptional passes\":\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.adapters.onebot11]\ndeny = true\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.users.onebot11]\nallow = [\"u_admin\"]   # Even if the adapter-level policy is to deny, events from u_admin are still allowed\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "61297e45514aa7389a6c01c3292e8e29",
      "translation": "## ③ Command-level (Command ACL)\n\nAnswer the question \"Who can execute a specific command?\" The decision order is: **deny hit → deny; allow whitelist is not empty and not hit → deny; neither configured → follow `default_allow`** (`true` gives the default permission chain to developers). Commands that are denied will explicitly reply with \"Insufficient permissions\".\n\n- Command names support glob: `\"roll*\"` covers a family of commands such as `roll` and `roll_dice` with a single rule.\n- Exact keys take precedence over glob keys (`commands.roll` will be matched and `commands.\"roll*\"` will not be checked).\n- User identifiers are in the format `\"platform:user_id\"` (consistent with the framework's owner system).\n- This dimension is **only an additional gate on the user side** and is chained with the command's `master` / `permission` parameters:\n  After ACL passes, it still follows the default permission chain declared by the developer (this default chain can be overridden and adjusted using ⑤)."
    },
    {
      "hash": "e33358d1dac070ba3a47efd9e311f43d",
      "translation": "## ④ Processor/Text Dimension\n\nFilter \"what text\" by module: After configuring `pattern` / `regex` for a module, all event handlers of that module only trigger when the text matches (AND with conditions in the code, both must be satisfied). This is suitable for narrowing the trigger scope of a module without modifying its code.\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.handlers.ChatModule]\npattern = \"闲聊*\"     # Event handlers of ChatModule only respond to messages starting with \"闲聊\"\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "da4016147ae0042c2b6b35ae3d95bd5c",
      "translation": "## ⑤ Implement Parameter Override\n\nOverride implementation parameters at the **upper level** of module/command registration, without modifying the module code:\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.overrides.MyModule.restart]\nmaster = true      # Override to allow only the framework owner (can also be set to false to relax the owner restriction for developers)\nhidden = true      # Hide from help list\naliases = [\"rs\"]   # Effective alias\n```\n\n> Override follows the principle of **user priority**: The `master` / `hidden` values declared by the developer are only default values. When the user explicitly configures them here, the user's configuration takes precedence (can tighten or loosen restrictions).  \n> Overrides only affect **implementation parameters** (`master` / `hidden` / `aliases` / `prefix` / `help` / `usage`, etc.). Command execution decisions and help rendering share the same merged result: `hidden` override immediately changes the visibility in the help list, and `help` / `usage` override immediately changes the display in `/help`.  \n> **Disabling a command is not done here**—use command-level deny (`scope.commands` or `scope.deny_user()`), to avoid conflicting meanings of \"disable\" from two different mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "hash": "c4b1b745b017aa4447d000100966cc43",
      "translation": "## ⑥ Outbound Action Dimension (Prohibition Module Initiating Outbound Calls)\n\nRestricts **outbound actions initiated by modules**: message sending / standard API actions / request operations.  \nThe three action types correspond to the underlying DSL: `Event.reply` and `Send` (`send`), `Api` / `call_api` (`api`), and `Request`'s `accept`/`reject` (`request`). Outbound calls initiated by modules during event handler execution carry the module owner, which is uniformly determined by this dimension.\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.actions.MyModule]\nsend = false      # Prohibit MyModule from replying/actively sending messages\napi = false       # Prohibit MyModule from calling standard API actions (including call escape pod)\nrequest = false   # Prohibit MyModule from executing accept/reject on request events\n```\n\nSemantic of judgment: **Default is fully allowed**—calls with unconfigured settings or empty owner (internal framework layer calls) are permitted; only when explicitly set to `false` by the user is the call rejected. Rejected calls do not initiate any network requests and directly return a standard failure response (`retcode = 34601`, see [api-response §5.3](../standards/api-response.md#53-framework-extended-return-codes-34xxx-customized-low-three-digits-of-the-platform-error-segment)). The three actions are independent, allowing individual restrictions.\n\n```python\n# Runtime API\nsdk.scope.set_action(\"MyModule\", \"send\", False)   # Prohibit message sending\nsdk.scope.is_action_allowed(\"MyModule\", \"send\")   # False\nsdk.scope.unset_action(\"MyModule\", \"send\")        # Restore permission\nsdk.scope.get_action_rules(\"MyModule\")            # {\"send\": False, \"api\": True, \"request\": True}\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "f16d763ae27347a6103c017890eca064",
      "translation": "## Runtime API\n\n### Module Dimension\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse import sdk\n\n# Check permission\nsdk.scope.is_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"Chat\")\nsdk.scope.is_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"Chat\", \"789012345\")\nsdk.scope.is_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", None)      # Framework-level resource -> True\n\n# Bind / Unbind\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", modules=[\"Chat\", \"Tool*\"])\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", blocked=[\"Danger\"])             # Platform-level\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"789012345\", modules=[\"Chat\"])  # Session-level\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", modules=[\"Music\"], merge=True)  # Merge\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", modules=[\"Chat\"], persist=False)  # Runtime only\nsdk.scope.unbind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\")\n\n# Query\nsdk.scope.get(\"onebot11\", \"123456\")   # {\"modules\": [\"Chat\"], \"blocked\": []}\n```\n\n### Identity Dimension\n\n```python\n# Check if event is allowed\nsdk.scope.is_identity_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"group_9\", \"u1\")\n\n# Bind strategy (priority determined by parameters: user > session > bot > adapter)\nsdk.scope.bind_identity(\"onebot11\", user_id=\"u_bad\", deny=True)\nsdk.scope.bind_identity(\"onebot11\", user_id=\"spam_*\", deny=True)   # glob\nsdk.scope.bind_identity(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"group_9\", allow=True)\nsdk.scope.unbind_identity(\"onebot11\", user_id=\"u_bad\")\n\n# User blacklist convenience API\nsdk.scope.block_user(\"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\nsdk.scope.is_user_blocked(\"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\nsdk.scope.get_blocked_users()        # {\"onebot11\": [\"u_bad\"]}\nsdk.scope.unblock_user(\"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\n```\n\n### Command Dimension\n\n```python\nsdk.scope.is_command_allowed(\"roll\", \"onebot11\", \"u1\")\nsdk.scope.allow_user(\"roll*\", \"onebot11\", \"u_vip\")   # Command name supports glob\nsdk.scope.deny_user(\"roll*\", \"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\nsdk.scope.get_acl(\"roll*\")\nsdk.scope.remove_acl(\"roll*\")\n\n# Can also use command system facade (equivalent delegation)\nfrom ErisPulse.Core.Event import command\ncommand.allow_user(\"restart\", \"onebot11\", \"123456\")\n```\n\n### Handler and Override Dimension\n\n```python\nsdk.scope.bind_handler(\"MyModule\", pattern=\"签到*\", regex=r\"\\d+号\")\nsdk.scope.unbind_handler(\"MyModule\")\n\nsdk.scope.override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\", master=True, hidden=True)\nsdk.scope.get_override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\")\nsdk.scope.remove_override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\")\n```\n\n### General\n\n```python\nsdk.scope.list_bindings()   # All bindings\nsdk.scope.get_topology()    # Topology (for Dashboard)\nsdk.scope.get_stats()\n# {\"module_calls\": .., \"module_filtered\": .., \"identity_checks\": .., \"identity_denied\": ..,\n#  \"command_checks\": .., \"command_denied\": .., \"action_checks\": .., \"action_denied\": ..,\n#  \"cache_hits\": .., \"cache_misses\": ..}\nsdk.scope.reset_stats()\nsdk.scope.clear()           # Clear all bindings (in-memory only)\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "ce25e28c9a26feb3dfa64812728fe107",
      "translation": "## Master Identity and Custom Identity Providers\n\nThe master system answers the question \"Who is the framework master?\": The `master=True` parameter for commands and the `master.is_master()` method in the business layer share the same identity verification chain, which consists of **configuration master → runtime record → provider chain**.\n\nMaster configuration (`ErisPulse.master.users`, supports both global list and platform-specific dict) is detailed in the [Configuration Guide](../user-guide/configuration.md#Master-System-Configuration). This section focuses on identity verification APIs and extension points.\n\n### Verification and Runtime Additions/Removals\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse.Core import master\n\nmaster.is_master(event)                      # Verify from event\nmaster.is_master(\"yunhu\", \"123\")             # Explicit verification\nmaster.add(\"yunhu\", \"123\")                   # Add at runtime (default: persistent; persist=False for in-memory only)\nmaster.remove(\"yunhu\", \"123\")                # Remove (default: persistent)\nmaster.list()                                # Summary: {\"global\": [...], \"<platform>\": [...]}\n```\n\n### Custom Identity Sources (Providers)\n\nIn addition to configuration, you can register custom identity sources: `fn(platform, user_id) -> bool`. These are tried in order when built-in identity sources (configuration + runtime records) do not match; if any provider grants permission, the user is considered a master. This is suitable for integrating with adapter administrator interfaces, database roles, or other external identity systems.\n\nThe registration entry `master.provider` supports both decorator and function-based syntax. Unregistering is done uniformly through the `fn.unregister()` method attached to the registered function:\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse.Core import master\n\n# Method 1: Decorator (persistent identity source, recommended)\n@master.provider\ndef admin_provider(platform, user_id):\n    return user_id in {\"999\"}     # Custom verification logic\n\nmaster.is_master(\"yunhu\", \"999\")   # True\nadmin_provider.unregister()        # Unregister when no longer needed\n\n# Method 2: Function-based (register during module loading / unregister during unloading)\nfn = master.provider(admin_provider)\nfn.unregister()\n```\n\n> Provider exceptions are caught and skipped, without interrupting the identity verification chain. Bound instance methods cannot attach `unregister`; for scenarios requiring paired registration/unregistration, use **module-level functions**.\n\n### User Priority: Master Scope is Ultimately Determined by the User\n\nThe command's `master=True` is merely a **default set by the developer**: users can override or relax this in the control panel via `ErisPulse.scope.overrides.<module>.<cmd>.master = true/false` (see above ⑤ Implementation of Parameter Override, where explicit user configuration takes effect)."
    },
    {
      "hash": "a6bc5232fec23d25f8eeabb663b53ece",
      "translation": "## Caching and Hot Updates\n\n- The results of `is_allowed` / `is_identity_allowed` are cached with **LRU caching** (`scope.cache_size` is adjustable),\n  and are automatically invalidated by `bind_*` / `unbind_*` / configuration hot updates (`config.updated` / `config.set`).\n- All dimension configurations take effect **immediately** without requiring a restart.\n- The control plane performs \"per-event\" evaluation without cross-event memory: if the configuration changes, the next event will be evaluated using the new rules."
    },
    {
      "hash": "7081718e954a534339fcd42e44f47c1d",
      "translation": "## Frequently Asked Questions and Precautions\n\n### 1. Configuration Hierarchy and Overriding\n\n- **Module Level**: Session > Bot > Platform, with **overall overriding**. If you want \"Chat allowed at the platform level, and Music added at the Bot level,\" you must list both at the Bot level.\n- **Identity Level**: User > Session > Bot > Adapter, taking the **most specific** configured policy (exceptions can be allowed).\n- **Command Level**: Exact command names take precedence over glob keys.\n\n### 2. Use the Control Plane Instead of Modifying Module Code\n\nModules declare \"developer defaults\" (`master=True`, `permission=...`, `pattern=...`); the control plane declares \"user final decisions.\" Parameter overrides follow the **user priority** principle:\n- Explicitly configured `master = true/false` by the user takes effect directly (can tighten or loosen).\n- Restrictions not set by the developer can be tightened by the user; disable/allow control is handled through the `deny` command and identity `allow`.\n\n### 3. Module/Command Not Responding\n\nFirst suspect the control plane rather than the module itself:\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse import sdk\n\nprint(sdk.scope.is_allowed(event.get_platform(), bot_id, \"MyModule\", session_id))\nprint(sdk.scope.is_identity_allowed(event.get_platform(), bot_id, session_id, user_id))\nprint(sdk.scope.get_stats())   # module_filtered / identity_denied > 0 indicates silent filtering\n```\n\nFiltering is **silent** (no response at module or identity levels to avoid exposing rules), but statistics will accumulate; commands denied by ACL will explicitly reply with \"insufficient permissions.\"\n\n### 4. Session Identifiers Are Isolated Across Platforms\n\nThe combination `(platform, session_id)` is the unique identifier. `scope.sessions.onebot11.\"789\"` only applies to onebot11 and does not affect a session with the same `789` on Telegram. The same applies to user keys at the identity level."
    },
    {
      "hash": "78e2f3a4867d57014620cc9163a93f17",
      "translation": "## Topology Tree API\n\n`ModuleManager.get_topology()` and `AdapterManager.get_topology()` provide data on module/adapter ownership relationships. `sdk.get_topology()` aggregates (including control plane `scope` with five dimensions) in one call:\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse import sdk\n\ntopology = sdk.get_topology()\n# {\n#   \"modules\": {                                   # Module → Owned resources\n#     \"Chat\": {\n#       \"loaded\": True, \"enabled\": True,\n#       \"commands\": [\"chat\", \"translate\"],\n#       \"handlers\": {\"message\": 2, \"notice\": 1},\n#       \"routes\": {\"http\": [\"/Chat/api\"], \"ws\": [], \"sse\": []},\n#       \"lifecycle_hooks\": 3,\n#     }\n#   },\n#   \"adapters\": {                                  # Adapter → Bot → Scope\n#     \"onebot11\": {\n#       \"status\": \"started\", \"enabled\": True,\n#       \"bots\": {\"123456\": {\"status\": \"online\", \"scope\": {...}}},\n#       \"scope\": {\"modules\": [...], \"blocked\": [...]},\n#     }\n#   },\n#   \"scope\": {                                     # Unified control plane (five dimensions)\n#     \"platforms\": {...}, \"bots\": {...}, \"sessions\": {...},\n#     \"identity\": {\"adapters\": {...}, \"bots\": {...}, \"sessions\": {...}, \"users\": {...}},\n#     \"commands\": {...}, \"handlers\": {...}, \"overrides\": {...},\n#   },\n# }\n```\n\n- The module topology aggregates commands, event handlers, HTTP/WS/SSE routes, and lifecycle hooks registered by the module, making it easier to visualize the module resource tree.\n- The adapter topology aggregates the status of each adapter, the status of its subordinate Bots, and platform-level/Bot-level scope bindings."
    }
  ]
}